<section id="problem-finding-story" class="section border-t border-line">
  <div class="container-page">
    <div class="reveal max-w-[62rem]">
      <p class="eyebrow">01</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">How do you find the real problem?</h2>
      <div class="prose-hera mt-6 max-w-[50rem]">
        <p>
          I usually start by asking one thing:
          <strong>what is the user carrying that the product should be carrying instead?</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="story-carousel reveal" data-story-carousel tabindex="0">
      <button class="nav-arrow prev" type="button" data-prev aria-label="Previous idea" hidden>←</button>

      <div class="slides" aria-live="polite">

        <article class="slide active" data-slide="0" aria-hidden="false">
          <div class="slide1-wrapper">
            <div class="slide1-layout">
              <div class="slide1-copy">
                <p class="kicker">The HERA story</p>
                <p class="slide1-bold">
                  I thought the problem was managing agents. It turned out the problem was everything I had to remember for them.
                </p>
                <p class="slide1-body">
                  AI agents could already ask questions, use tools, and work for long periods. But I was still remembering which file was current, what I had already decided, what another agent had learned, which version had been reviewed, and whether I had answered the same question before.
                </p>
                <p class="slide1-body">
                  HERA started because I kept feeling that exact problem while working with AI.
                </p>
              </div>

